> The 'gan zha' (干炸) technique is all about patience—dry frying the ground pork over medium-high heat until it releases all its moisture and becomes beautifully crumbly with crispy edges. This transforms ordinary ground pork into something with incredible depth and texture. The crispy pork pairs wonderfully with the tender greens.

== Dry-Fry the Pork ==

Heat a #wok{} over medium-high heat until smoking slightly. Add @ground pork{400%g} and break it up with a spatula.

Cook the pork, stirring occasionally and pressing it against the wok to 'dry fry' it. After ~{5%minutes}, the pork will release its liquid and start to sizzle in its own fat. Continue cooking, stirring and breaking up any clumps, for another ~{8-10%minutes}.

The pork is ready when it becomes crumbly, slightly crispy, and emits a fragrant, roasted aroma. There should be no liquid left in the wok—only the pork and some rendered fat. Remove the pork to a bowl and set aside.

== Prepare the Aromatics ==

In the same wok over medium heat, add @oil{1%tbsp} and @butter{1%tbsp}.

Add @minced ginger{15%g} and @minced garlic{15%g}. Stir-fry for ~{30%seconds} until fragrant.

Add @bacon{50%g}(finely diced) or @lardons{50%g} and cook for ~{2%minutes} until the bacon renders its fat and becomes slightly crispy [- This adds an extra layer of savory depth -].

== Assemble the Dish ==

Separate the @bok choy{3%heads} leaves. Cut the thicker white stems into 3 cm pieces and keep the leaves whole.

Add the bok choy stems to the wok and stir-fry for ~{2%minutes}. Add the leaves and continue cooking for ~{1%minute} until just wilted.

Create a well in the center of the wok. Add @oyster sauce{1.5%tbsp}, @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, and @water{3%tbsp}. Let the sauce bubble for ~{30%seconds}.

Return the crispy pork to the wok. Toss everything together over high heat for ~{1%minute} until the pork is well incorporated and the sauce has coated everything.

== Finishing ==

If using @tofu{200%g}(firm, cubed), pan-fry in a separate #frying pan{} until golden on the outside, then add to the wok for extra protein [- This is optional but adds wonderful texture -].

Finish with @sesame oil{1%tsp}, @scallions{4}(julienned), and a light sprinkle of @salt{} to taste.

Serve immediately over steamed rice or with noodles.

== Variations ==

Other Dutch-available vegetables work beautifully here:
- Substitute @Chinese cabbage{300%g}(wombok, shredded) for the bok choy
- Use @snow peas{150%g} or @green beans{150%g} in addition to or instead of bok choy
- Add @mushrooms{150%g}(quartered) for extra umami